%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 98395f251812ec84d8bbc703ba333038, type: 3}
  m_Name: Stage2_Quiz
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  quizId: Q2
  stageId: 2
  topics:
  - id: path
    name: Typhoon path
  - id: mangroves
    name: Mangroves & storm surge
  - id: warmseas
    name: Warmer seas
  - id: bluecarbon
    name: Blue carbon
  - id: sierra
    name: The Sierra Madre
  - id: seas
    name: Rising seas
  - id: game
    name: Game check
  preQuestions:
  - questionId: S2_PRE_1
    topicId: path
    text: Why is the Pacific coast of Luzon, such as Aurora and Isabela, often the
      first land a typhoon reaches?
    choices:
    - Typhoons form over the West Philippine Sea and move east
    - Most typhoons form over the warm western Pacific Ocean and move west toward
      the Philippines
    - The Sierra Madre mountains pull typhoons toward them
    - "Typhoons form only near the coast during El Ni\xF1o years"
    correctIndex: 1
    scored: 1
  - questionId: S2_PRE_2
    topicId: mangroves
    text: How do mangrove forests protect coastal towns during a typhoon?
    choices:
    - They absorb rainwater so rivers do not flood
    - Their leaves block the wind so the typhoon weakens
    - They pull the storm away from the town
    - Their roots and trunks slow the waves and lower the storm surge
    correctIndex: 3
    scored: 1
  - questionId: S2_PRE_3
    topicId: warmseas
    text: How does warmer ocean water affect typhoons?
    choices:
    - It gives typhoons more energy, so they can become stronger
    - It cools the air above it, so typhoons weaken
    - It has no effect; typhoons get their energy only from the wind
    - It stops typhoons from forming
    correctIndex: 0
    scored: 1
  - questionId: S2_PRE_4
    topicId: bluecarbon
    text: What is "blue carbon"?
    choices:
    - Carbon dioxide dissolved in rainwater
    - Pollution from ships that turns the sea blue
    - Carbon stored by coastal plants such as mangroves and seagrass, much of it
      in the mud
    - The carbon in fish and other sea animals that people eat
    correctIndex: 2
    scored: 1
  - questionId: S2_PRE_5
    topicId: sierra
    text: What does the Sierra Madre mountain range do when a typhoon crosses it?
    choices:
    - It weakens the typhoon's winds somewhat, but rain can fall even harder on its
      western side
    - It blocks the typhoon completely, so western Luzon is safe
    - It turns the typhoon back out to sea
    - It has no effect on typhoons at all
    correctIndex: 0
    scored: 1
  - questionId: S2_PRE_6
    topicId: seas
    text: Green sea turtles nest on the beaches of Aurora. How does climate change
      threaten their nests?
    choices:
    - Warmer seas make the turtles stop swimming
    - Rising seas and stronger waves erode and wash away the beaches they nest on
    - Typhoon winds scare the turtles away from the Philippines for good
    - Climate change affects only land animals
    correctIndex: 1
    scored: 1
  postQuestions:
  - questionId: S2_POST_1
    topicId: path
    text: Why does a province on Luzon's eastern coast face more typhoon landfalls
      than one facing the West Philippine Sea?
    choices:
    - The west coast has colder water that typhoons cannot cross
    - Typhoons are drawn toward big cities in the east
    - The eastern coast has fewer trees, so storms are pulled there
    - It faces the Pacific, where most typhoons form and head west, so it is often
      hit first
    correctIndex: 3
    scored: 1
  - questionId: S2_POST_2
    topicId: mangroves
    text: A town's mangroves are cut down to build fishponds. What is most likely
      during the next typhoon?
    choices:
    - Waves and storm surge reach the town with more force
    - The town is safer, because the fishpond walls hold back the sea
    - Nothing changes, because only seawalls stop storm surges
    - The typhoon avoids the town, because there is less forest to attract it
    correctIndex: 0
    scored: 1
  - questionId: S2_POST_3
    topicId: warmseas
    text: Typhoons that hit East and Southeast Asia have grown stronger since the
      late 1970s. What best explains this?
    choices:
    - New mountains and cities changed the region's winds
    - Typhoons now form closer to the North Pole
    - The sea surface near these coasts has become warmer
    - Fewer typhoons form, so each one grows bigger by default
    correctIndex: 2
    scored: 1
  - questionId: S2_POST_4
    topicId: bluecarbon
    text: Why does clearing a mangrove forest harm the climate more than many people
      expect?
    choices:
    - Mangroves are the only plants near the sea that make oxygen
    - Mangroves store large amounts of carbon, much of it in the mud, and clearing
      releases it
    - Cleared mangroves make the sea colder
    - It harms only the fish, not the climate
    correctIndex: 1
    scored: 1
  - questionId: S2_POST_5
    topicId: sierra
    text: A typhoon heads for Aurora. A resident of Nueva Ecija, west of the Sierra
      Madre, says, "We're safe, the mountains will block it." What is the best reply?
    choices:
    - Right, typhoons never cross the Sierra Madre
    - Right, but only if the typhoon is weak
    - Wrong, the mountains make the typhoon stronger
    - 'Wrong: the mountains weaken the winds somewhat, but heavy rain and floods
      are still a danger'
    correctIndex: 3
    scored: 1
  - questionId: S2_POST_6
    topicId: seas
    text: Which of these is a climate change threat to coastal places like the beaches
      of Aurora?
    choices:
    - Beaches growing wider because the sea is drying up
    - Fewer fish because the water is getting too cold
    - Rising seas and erosion shrinking beaches and coastal habitats
    - The sea turning into fresh water from heavier rain
    correctIndex: 2
    scored: 1
  - questionId: S2_GAME_1
    topicId: game
    text: In the Sierra Madre Coast stage, what do the enemies stand for?
    choices:
    - Illegal fishermen and pirates
    - Only the typhoons that hit the coast
    - Coal and diesel smoke and polluted runoff that harm the coast and the climate
    - Tourists who litter on the beach
    correctIndex: 2
    scored: 0
  - questionId: S2_GAME_2
    topicId: game
    text: In the boss level, why does keeping Forest allies alive weaken the Typhoon
      Tyrant's Storm Surge?
    choices:
    - Standing forests slow typhoon winds and hold the soil during heavy rain
    - Forest allies have the most health in the game
    - Typhoons are afraid of trees
    - Forests stop rain from falling
    correctIndex: 0
    scored: 0
